COMMAND NAME: gpmigrator

Upgrades an existing Greenplum Database 3.3.x.x system to 4.0.x.x.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpmigrator <old_GPHOME_path> <new_GPHOME_path> 
           --replication_port_base <port> 
           --mirror_replication_port_base <port>  
           --mirror-mode {redundant | single}
           --gpperfmonhome <path>
           [--nosnapshot] [-d <master_data_directory>] 
           [-l <logfile_directory>] [-q] [--debug] [-R]

gpmigrator <old_GPHOME_path> <new_GPHOME_path> --resume

gpmigrator --version | -v

gpmigrator --help | -h


*****************************************************
PREREQUISITES
*****************************************************

The following tasks should be performed prior to executing 
an upgrade:

* Make sure you are logged in to the master host as the 
  Greenplum Database superuser (gpadmin).

* Install the Greenplum Database 4.0 binaries on all 
  Greenplum hosts.

* Copy any custom modules from your existing installation to 
  your 4.0 installation on all Greenplum hosts. For example, 
  shared library files for user-defined functions in $GPHOME/lib 
  or PostgreSQL add-on modules (such as plr.so or pgcrypto.so) 
  in $GPHOME/lib/postgresql.

* Copy or preserve any additional folders or files (such as 
  backup folders) that you have added in the Greenplum data 
  directories or $GPHOME directory. Only files or folders 
  strictly related to Greenplum Database operations are 
  preserved by the migration utility.

* (Optional) Run VACUUM on all databases, and remove old 
  server log files from pg_log in your master and segment 
  data directories. This is not required, but will reduce 
  the size of Greenplum Database files to be backed up and 
  migrated.

* Remove the gp_jetpack schema from all databases (if it exists). 
  Also note that gp_ is a reserved prefix for schema names in 
  Greenplum Database 4.0 (as is pg_). If you have named any 
  schemas with a gp_ prefix, Greenplum recommends that you 
  rename them prior to upgrading.

* The use of OIDs (per-row system-generated object identifiers) 
  has been disabled for partitioned and column-oriented tables 
  in 4.0. If you have these types of tables that were created 
  with OIDS=TRUE, you must alter them to be with OIDS=FALSE.

* Check for and recover any failed segments in your current 
  Greenplum Database system (gpstate, gprecoverseg).

* (Optional) Verify the integrity of your current system catalogs 
  using the gpcheckcat utility.

* (Optional, but highly recommended) Backup your current databases 
  (gpcrondump or ZFS snapshots). If you find any issues when testing 
  your upgraded system, you can restore this backup.

* Remove the standby master from your system configuration 
  (gpinitstandby -r).

* Do a clean shutdown of your current system (gpstop).

* Update your environment to source the 4.0 installation.

* Inform all database users of the upgrade and lockout time frame. 
  Once the upgrade is in process, users will not be allowed on 
  the system until the upgrade is complete.


IMPORTANT: Failing to meet any of these prerequisites can result 
           in an aborted upgrade, placing your system in an unusable 
           or even unrecoverable state. Make sure you meet all the 
           prerequisites before running gpmigrator.


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpmigrator utility upgrades an existing Greenplum Database 
3.3.x.x system to 4.0. This utility updates the system catalog 
and internal version number, but not the actual software binaries.

During the migration process, all client connections to the 
master will be locked out. The migration utility locks all 
client access to the master, however it does not block direct 
utility mode access to the segments. To ensure a safe upgrade, 
make sure users are aware that any connections to a Greenplum 
segment are not safe during the upgrade time frame.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

<old_GPHOME_path>

 Required. The absolute path to the current version of 
 Greenplum Database software you want to migrate away from.


<new_GPHOME_path>

 Required. The absolute path to the new version of Greenplum 
 Database software you want to migrate to.


--replication_port_base <port>

 Required if upgrading a system with mirrors. Specifies the base 
 number by which the port numbers for the primary file replication 
 process are calculated. The first replication port on a host is 
 set as REPLICATION_PORT_BASE, and then incremented by one for 
 each additional primary segment on that host. Valid values range 
 from 1 through 65535 and cannot conflict with other port assignments 
 on a segment host.


--mirror_replication_port_base <port>

 Required if upgrading a system with mirrors. Specifies the base 
 number by which the port numbers for the mirror file replication 
 process are calculated. The first mirror replication port on a 
 host is set as MIRROR_REPLICATION_PORT_BASE, and then incremented 
 by one for each additional mirror segment on that host. Valid 
 values range from 1 through 65535 and cannot conflict with other 
 port assignments on a segment host.


--mirror-mode {redundant | single}

 Required if upgrading a system with mirrors.
 Specifies how to upgrade mirror segments to the new 4.0 format. 
 Redundant mode will keep a copy of the old 3.3 mirror until its 
 new mirror is fully synchronized. Redundant mode always ensures 
 that the system always has a valid mirror copy for every segment, 
 and is the preferred mode provided that your systems have enough 
 available disk space. Single mode removes the old mirror before 
 creating the new mirror on the target node, and can be used on 
 systems where disk space is limited. All segments are upgraded 
 in parallel using the maximum parallelism allowed by the available 
 network, I/O, and CPU bandwidth.


--gpperfmonhome <path>

 Required only if your current system has the Greenplum 
 Performance Monitor installed and enabled. Provide the 
 full path to your 3.3.x Greenplum Performance Monitor 
 installation directory ($GPPERFMONHOME). The upgrade 
 process will stop the monitor data collection agents 
 and web application. You must then upgrade the Greenplum 
 Performance Monitor Web Application to 4.0 after upgrade 
 is complete.


--nosnapshot

 (Solaris only) On systems that support zfs snapshots the default 
 behavior is to pause the upgrade after the mirrors have been 
 synchronized, but before the upgraded catalog has been installed. 
 This allows users to take a zfs snapshot before upgrade progresses 
 beyond the point of no return. The --nosnapshot option runs the 
 complete upgrade as a single step, just as it would do on systems 
 that do not support zfs.


--resume

 (Solaris only) On systems that support zfs snapshots, allows 
 the upgrade process to resume where it left off after it has 
 been paused to allow for a zfs snapshot to be taken.


-R (revert)

 Restores the backup directory in the event of a failed 
 upgrade. This is only available if running in redundant 
 mode (mirror-mode=redundant). If the migration procedure fails 
 and you cannot start the system using the binary files of the 
 prior version, it is probable that gpmigrator was not able 
 to revert automatically. In these cases, you can run 
 gpmigrator -R to attempt to restore the backup directory. 
 Cases in which the migration is not automatically reverted 
 include:
  * The gpmigrator process is killed with a command such 
    as kill -9.
  * The system crashes during the migration procedure.
  * You are running gpmigrator in mirror-mode=single mode.
 If a ZFS snapshot is available, backing up from those 
 resources is preferred.


-d <master_data_directory>

 Optional. The current master host data directory. If not 
 specified, the value set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY is used.


-l <logfile_directory>

 The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-q (quiet mode)

 Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on 
 the screen, but is still written to the log file.


--help | -h

 Displays the online help.


--debug

 Sets logging level to debug.


--version | -v

 Displays the version of this utility. 


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Upgrade to version 4.0 from version 3.3.6.1 (make sure you 
are using the 4.0 version of gpmigrator): 

  /usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0/bin/gpmigrator \
    --replication_port_base 53000 \
    --mirror_replication_port_base 54000 \
    --mirror-mode redundant \
    --gpperfmonhome /usr/local/greenplum-perfmon-2.1.0.0 \
    /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.3.6.0 \
    /usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0


Resume an upgrade that has been paused for a snapshot 
(make sure you are using the 4.0 version of gpmigrator):

  /usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0/bin/gpmigrator \
    /usr/local/greenplum-db-3.3.6.1 \
    /usr/local/greenplum-db-4.0.0.0 --resume


